  "preview": "Two kayak rental shops use different pricing structures: one charges more for the first hour and less after, the other charges a flat per-hour rate. Where do they break even — and what should you tell a friend who only has a $40 budget?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Model a tiered (piecewise) pricing structure with a two-step equation, solve for an unknown number of hours given a total cost, and use the model to compare against a flat-rate alternative — including translating a budget constraint into an inequality.",
    "studentPrompt": "Pelican Cove rents kayaks at the lake. Their pricing is:\n\n- First hour: $14\n- Each additional hour: $8\n\n1. Carlos rents a kayak and ends up paying $54. How many hours did he have the kayak? Write an equation and solve it. Be sure to explain what your variable represents.\n2. Write a general formula for the total cost C of renting a kayak from Pelican Cove for h hours, where h is at least 1. Use your formula to check Carlos's bill.\n3. Across the lake, Heron Bay charges a flat $10 per hour with no first-hour upcharge. For what number of hours is Pelican Cove the better deal? Explain how you know — try the formula, a table, OR a graph, but be ready to defend your choice.\n4. Carlos's little sister has only $40 to spend. Write an INEQUALITY that describes how many hours she can rent from Pelican Cove without going over budget. Solve it. What's the longest rental she can afford? (Bonus: same question for Heron Bay.)",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A flat illustration of two kayaks on a calm lake at sunrise, one orange and one teal, with a small dock and a price sign in the foreground.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"dawn\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#fed7aa\"/><stop offset=\"0.6\" stop-color=\"#fdba74\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#fbbf24\"/></linearGradient><linearGradient id=\"lake\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#7dd3fc\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#1e40af\"/></linearGradient></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"110\" fill=\"url(#dawn)\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"110\" width=\"480\" height=\"110\" fill=\"url(#lake)\"/><circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"95\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" opacity=\"0.95\"/><path d=\"M0,108 Q40,104 80,108 T160,108 T240,108 T320,108 T400,108 T480,108\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fef3c7\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><path d=\"M0,118 Q60,114 120,118 T240,118 T360,118 T480,118\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\"0.6\"/><path d=\"M0,140 Q70,136 140,140 T280,140 T420,140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#bae6fd\" stroke-width=\"1\" opacity=\"0.5\"/><g transform=\"translate(110,150)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"55\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#ea580c\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"-2\" rx=\"45\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"#fb923c\"/><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-6\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#1f2937\"/><line x1=\"-25\" y1=\"-12\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"-12\" stroke=\"#78350f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"-25\" cy=\"-12\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"3\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/><ellipse cx=\"25\" cy=\"-12\" rx=\"5\" ry=\"3\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(300,175)\"><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#0d9488\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"-2\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"3.5\" fill=\"#14b8a6\"/><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-5\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1f2937\"/><line x1=\"-22\" y1=\"-10\" x2=\"22\" y2=\"-10\" stroke=\"#78350f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"-22\" cy=\"-10\" rx=\"4\" ry=\"2.5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\"/><ellipse cx=\"22\" cy=\"-10\" rx=\"4\" ry=\"2.5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(380,135)\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"55\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#fefce8\" stroke=\"#78350f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#78350f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#78350f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">PELICAN COVE</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"7\" fill=\"#1f2937\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1st hr: $14</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"7\" fill=\"#1f2937\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+hr: $8</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"6\" fill=\"#6b7280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">KAYAKS</text></g><rect x=\"0\" y=\"195\" width=\"50\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#92400e\"/><rect x=\"5\" y=\"160\" width=\"40\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#a16207\"/><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#78350f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#78350f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "If your kids have ever rented anything — bikes, scooters, bowling shoes — pull on that. Tell the story. Ask: 'If the first hour is $14 and each more hour is $8, how much for 5 hours?' Take a quick guess from the room. Then turn them loose on the four questions.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: graph paper or graphing tool for Q3"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "two-step-equations",
      "linear-models",
      "tiered-pricing",
      "inequalities",
      "break-even",
      "comparison"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Low floor: kids can guess-and-check Carlos's hours quickly. Diamond is Q2 (writing a clean formula that handles the 'first hour is different' structure) and Q3 (the comparison answer is counterintuitive — flat $10/hour beats Pelican only for short rentals). Q4 brings in inequalities cleanly.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1 guess-and-check: 'After 1 hour = $14. After 2 hours = $22. After 3 = $30. After 4 = $38. After 5 = $46. After 6 = $54.' So 6 hours. Strong groups write 14 + 8(h − 1) = 54 and solve.\n- Q2 formula variants:\n   • C = 14 + 8(h − 1)\n   • C = 8h + 6  (equivalent, after distributing)\n   • Piecewise — some groups will write C = 14 if h = 1, C = 14 + 8(h−1) if h > 1.\n- Q3 (the diamond): Set Pelican = Heron. 8h + 6 = 10h → 6 = 2h → h = 3. So they cost the same at exactly 3 hours. For h < 3, Heron is cheaper; for h > 3, Pelican is cheaper. Many groups will assume Pelican is always cheaper because $8 < $10. The 'first-hour premium' is the trap.\n- Q4: 14 + 8(h − 1) ≤ 40 → 8h + 6 ≤ 40 → 8h ≤ 34 → h ≤ 4.25. Since rentals are by the hour (probably), longest rental is 4 hours ($38). Heron: 10h ≤ 40 → h ≤ 4. Also 4 hours, costing exactly $40.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- For Q1, treating the formula as 14h instead of 14 + 8(h−1). They get h = 54/14 ≈ 3.86 and either round wrong or get stuck.\n- For Q2, forgetting that the $14 includes the first hour, leading to C = 14 + 8h (no subtraction). Check: this gives $22 for 1 hour instead of $14.\n- For Q3, assuming whichever has the lower per-hour rate wins, ignoring the start-up premium.\n- For Q4, writing an equation (=) instead of an inequality (≤), or solving 14 + 8h ≤ 40 (the same Q2 error).",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups wrote different formulas for Q2 — C = 14 + 8(h − 1) and C = 8h + 6. Are they the same? Show it.\n- Pelican Cove charges MORE per hour after the first hour ($8) than Heron Bay's flat rate ($10) is — wait, that's backwards. Pelican charges LESS per additional hour. So why isn't Pelican always cheaper? What's the 'extra cost' you're paying at Pelican?\n- In Q4, the answer was h ≤ 4.25. We rounded down to 4. Why? When can you round h DOWN to be safe, and when does rounding down give the wrong answer?\n- Heron Bay's longest rental at $40 is exactly 4 hours, with zero cents to spare. Pelican's is also 4 hours, but the bill is only $38. Which deal is 'better' for the sister, and what does 'better' mean here?",
    "extensions": "- Pelican Cove is considering changing their pricing to '$10 first hour, $9 each additional hour' to compete with Heron Bay. Under the new pricing, when do the two companies cost the same? Is the new pricing better for Pelican (more revenue) for a typical 3-hour rental?\n- A third company, Otter Outfitters, charges $20 flat for up to 2 hours and $5 per additional hour. Compare all three companies on the same graph. For each company, find the range of rental times for which it is the cheapest option.\n- Pelican offers a 'group rate': 14 + 8(h − 1) for the first kayak, plus $6 per hour for each additional kayak (no first-hour premium on extras). A family of 4 rents 4 kayaks for 5 hours. What's the bill? Write a general formula for n kayaks and h hours.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Let h = total hours of rental. Equation: 14 + 8(h − 1) = 54. Solve: 14 + 8h − 8 = 54 → 8h + 6 = 54 → 8h = 48 → h = 6. Carlos had the kayak for 6 hours.\n2. C = 14 + 8(h − 1), or equivalently C = 8h + 6, for h ≥ 1. Check: h = 6 → C = 8(6) + 6 = 54. ✓\n3. Set 8h + 6 = 10h → 6 = 2h → h = 3 hours. At exactly 3 hours, both companies cost $30. For h > 3, Pelican is cheaper (its lower additional-hour rate wins out over the first-hour premium). For h < 3 (just a 1- or 2-hour rental), Heron Bay is cheaper.\n4. Inequality: 14 + 8(h − 1) ≤ 40, equivalently 8h + 6 ≤ 40 → 8h ≤ 34 → h ≤ 4.25. Since rentals are by the hour, the longest she can afford at Pelican is 4 hours (bill = $38). Bonus: Heron Bay → 10h ≤ 40 → h ≤ 4. Also 4 hours, but the bill is exactly $40.",
